// std/httpd.jtr — an HTTP/1.1 server above the parser: routing, middleware, keep-alive,
// timeouts, streaming responses, static files and an access log.
//
// TIER: `sys`, by dependency. Every byte in and out goes through `std/sysnet`; readiness comes
// from `std/syspoll`; files from `std/fs`; time from a `time.Clock` the server is GIVEN.
//
// ─── The shape: one thread, one poll, a table of connections ─────────────────────────
//
// The server is single-threaded and readiness-driven. A `step` asks the poller which of the
// listener and the live connections can be read without blocking, then does exactly that
// much work: accepts what is waiting, reads what has arrived, answers every complete request
// in the buffer, and closes what has timed out. Nothing here blocks except the poll itself,
// and the poll is bounded by the caller's budget.
//
// That is a deliberate choice over threads or `spawn`. `spawn` may not take a `mut`
// parameter — the language refuses the shared-state race a per-connection worker would
// need — so a thread per connection would have to be built out of channels, and a server
// whose requests arrive through channels is a different, larger module. One loop is also
// what makes the suite EXACT: client and server take turns in one process, so a test
// asserts a transcript rather than a race.
//
// ─── Handlers are function pointers with a context, and there is ONE of them per route ──
//
// A handler is `fn(*mut u8, mut Exchange) -> i32` plus the `*mut u8` it is called with — the
// `runtime.Task` shape, for `runtime`'s reason: a closure that captures cannot be stored, a
// fn pointer can. Middleware is the same shape in a list, run in order before routing; one
// answering `HTTPD_DONE` has written the response and routing is skipped.
//
// ─── Keep-alive, and why the buffer is per connection ─────────────────────────────────
//
// HTTP/1.1 keeps the connection unless a side says `Connection: close`; HTTP/1.0 closes
// unless it says `keep-alive`. A client may PIPELINE: send the next request before reading
// the answer to the last. So each connection owns a buffer, the parser reports how many bytes
// a message consumed (`req.total`), and the loop answers every complete message it holds
// before asking the kernel for more — the bytes after one request are the start of the next.
//
// ─── Two timeouts, because they are two different failures ───────────────────────────
//
// A READ timeout closes a connection that has sent PART of a request and stopped: that is
// Slowloris, and the parser's bounds (`HTTP_TOO_LARGE`) close only the memory half of it. An
// IDLE timeout closes a kept-alive connection nobody is using, so a table slot is not held
// forever by a client that went away without saying so. They are separate knobs with
// separate answers (408 for the first, a silent close for the second) because a client that
// stalled mid-request and one that finished and left are not the same client.
//
// ─── Streaming responses ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `respond` sends a body it is given whole. `stream_begin`/`stream_write`/`stream_end` send
// `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` and one chunk per write, for a body whose size is not known
// up front. The chunk framing is written here and decoded by `std/http` — the same module
// that refuses to DECODE anything ambiguous, so what this emits is what the parser accepts.
//
// ─── Streaming a request body IN: the sink, and why it is not a `read` call ──────────
//
// A body larger than the connection buffer cannot be handed to a handler whole, and a
// handler that BLOCKS asking for the next piece would stop the one thread every other
// connection is polled on — a slow uploader would starve the server, which is the whole
// failure this design exists to avoid. So a body is PUSHED, not pulled: `route_stream`
// registers a route with a `BodySink`, and the readiness loop hands the sink whatever
// arrived, in the poll, and returns. There is no blocking read anywhere and no coroutine to
// need one.
//
// The head is PINNED. While a body streams, the request's head sits at the front of the
// connection buffer and is never moved, so every accessor on the exchange keeps working and
// the body window is what is left of the buffer. Each round: read into the window, hand the
// sink what decoded, slide the undecoded remainder back against the head. When the body
// ends, whatever follows it in the buffer is the next request and is moved to the front —
// **that is the pipelining invariant, and it is the same memmove the buffered path does.**
//
//   `on_open(ctx, read Exchange) -> i32`   the head is in; 0 accepts, a status refuses
//   `on_data(ctx, read []u8) -> i32`       one piece; 0 accepts, a status refuses
//
// `on_open` takes the exchange by `read`, so a sink can look at the path, the parameters and
// every header and refuse on any of them — and cannot write a response, because the response
// belongs to the route's handler, which runs ONCE when the body is complete. A sink that
// wants to say something says it with a status. `body(x)` is "" for a streamed request (the
// bytes went to the sink, not to a buffer) and `body_streamed(x)` is how many DECODED bytes
// there were — the payload, not the chunk framing that carried it.
//
// Both framings stream. `Content-Length` delivers exactly that many bytes and not one more,
// so the bytes after it are the next request and never the body's; a client that promises
// more than it sends stalls, and the READ timeout — the same one Slowloris meets — closes it
// with a 408. `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` is decoded by `std/http`'s `chunk_step`, one unit
// per call over a buffer being refilled, and a malformed chunk size is a `400` and a close
// rather than a wait for bytes that would never make it valid.
//
// ─── Bounds, and what happens to a body nobody reads ─────────────────────────────────
//
// `set_max_body` bounds a request body — **counted as bytes RECEIVED, not bytes decoded**,
// because chunk framing is the client's choice and counting only the payload would let the
// overhead run forever. A declared `Content-Length` over the bound is `413` before one body
// byte is waited for; a chunked body that grows past it is `413` mid-stream.
//
// **A body a handler does not read** has four answers, and they are different on purpose:
//
//   * On an ordinary route, a body that FITS the buffer is parsed as part of the message, so
//     a handler that ignores it costs nothing and the connection carries on — the bytes were
//     already consumed, and pipelining is unaffected.
//   * On an ordinary route, a body that does NOT fit the buffer is `413` and **the connection
//     is closed**. It is not drained. Draining is work the client chose and the server did
//     not, and the only bound on how long it would take is the client's own honesty.
//   * A sink that refuses (`on_open` or `on_data` answering non-zero) gets that status sent
//     and **the connection closed**, for the same reason: the rest of a body that was
//     refused is not worth reading.
//   * A head that does not fit the buffer at all is still `431` — that is a header bound, and
//     a client told `413` would trim the wrong thing.
//
// Every one of those closes LINGERS first, and it has to: closing a socket that still holds
// unread bytes makes the kernel send a RESET, and the reset destroys the response that was
// just written to explain the refusal — so the client half way through an upload it should
// stop sending would see a dropped connection and no reason. `linger_close` discards what has
// ALREADY arrived, each read gated on a zero-timeout poll and the whole thing capped at
// `HTTPD_LINGER_READS` bufferfuls. That is still not the drain refused above: its cost is
// bounded by the server's own numbers, and the client cannot extend it by sending more.
//
// ─── What is deliberately absent ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **No TLS** (area 8). **No body streaming OUT of a handler on demand** — `stream_write` is
// push, same as in. **No `Expect: 100-continue`**: a client that waits for it gets no answer
// until the read timeout, which is the honest failure for a thing this server does not
// implement. **No HTTP/1.0 keep-alive negotiation beyond the header.** **No compression, no
// ranges, no ETags** for static files.
//
// Worked example:  examples/std/httpd_demo.jtr
// Run the tests:   jestyrc test examples/std/httpd_test.jtr

import "fs"
import "http"
import "list"
import "log"
import "mem"
import "runtime"
import "sink"
import "sysnet"
import "syspoll"
import "time"

pub const HTTPD_NEXT: i32 = 0      // a middleware answer: keep going
pub const HTTPD_DONE: i32 = 1      // a middleware answer: the response is written, stop here

// ---- handlers ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@copy pub struct Handler {
    pub ctx: *mut u8,
    pub f: fn(*mut u8, mut Exchange) -> i32,
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn handler(ctx: *mut u8, f: fn(*mut u8, mut Exchange) -> i32) -> Handler {
    return Handler{ ctx: ctx, f: f }
}

// ---- the exchange ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// One request and its response, handed to middleware and to the route's handler. The request
// bytes are the connection's buffer; every accessor is a view into it, valid for the length
// of the call. The response goes to the socket as it is written.

pub struct Exchange {
    buf: *mut u8,
    have: usize,
    req: http.Request,
    hs: *mut http.Header,
    nhs: usize,
    bbuf: *mut u8,              // the DECODED body when the request was chunked, else null
    blen: usize,
    streamed: i64,              // body bytes handed to a sink, for a streamed request
    route: i32,                 // the matched route's index, -1 before routing / when none
    params: List(i32),          // 4 per param: name off, name len (in the router's patterns),
                                //              value off, value len (in `buf`)
    pats: *mut u8,              // the router's pattern bytes, for the param names
    plen: usize,
    path: http.Span,
    query: http.Span,
    fd: i64,
    status: i32,
    hdrs: String,               // extra headers, already rendered as `Name: value\r\n`
    sent: bool,
    streaming: bool,
    bytes_out: i64,
    keep_alive: bool,
    obuf: *mut u8,
    ocap: usize,
    // The transport hook: when `wctx` is non-null every response byte goes through `wfn`
    // instead of the descriptor. `std/httpds` sets it to a TLS session; nothing else does.
    wctx: *mut u8,
    wfn: fn(*mut u8, read []u8) -> bool,
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn no_write(ctx: *mut u8, read b: []u8) -> bool { return false }

// ---- reading the request ----

pub fn method(read x: Exchange) -> str {
    let b: []u8 = slice(u8, x.buf, x.have)
    return http.span_str(x.req.method, b)
}

// The target up to the `?`.
pub fn path(read x: Exchange) -> str {
    let b: []u8 = slice(u8, x.buf, x.have)
    return http.span_str(x.path, b)
}

// Everything after the `?`, or "".
pub fn query(read x: Exchange) -> str {
    let b: []u8 = slice(u8, x.buf, x.have)
    return http.span_str(x.query, b)
}

// The first header named `name` (give it lower-case), or "".
pub fn header(read x: Exchange, read name: str) -> str {
    let b: []u8 = slice(u8, x.buf, x.have)
    let hs: []http.Header = slice(http.Header, x.hs, x.nhs)
    return http.header_value(hs, x.nhs, b, name)
}

// The request body, decoded. `str`, so a body that is not UTF-8 reads as "" — `body_len`
// still reports its length, which is how a caller tells "empty" from "not text".
pub fn body(read x: Exchange) -> str {
    if x.bbuf != null {
        let d: []u8 = slice(u8, x.bbuf, x.blen)
        let r = try_from_utf8(d)
        if is_err(r) { return "" }
        return unwrap(r)
    }
    let b: []u8 = slice(u8, x.buf, x.have)
    let r2 = try_from_utf8(b[x.req.body.off .. x.req.body.off + x.req.body.len])
    if is_err(r2) { return "" }
    return unwrap(r2)
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn body_len(read x: Exchange) -> usize {
    if x.bbuf != null { return x.blen }
    return x.req.body.len
}

// How many body bytes went to this route's sink. Zero for every request that was not
// streamed — a handler that wants to tell the two apart asks this and `body_len`.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn body_streamed(read x: Exchange) -> i64 { return x.streamed }

// ---- body sinks ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The consumer of a streamed request body. `@copy` and fn pointers, the `Handler` shape, for
// the same reason: a closure that captures cannot be stored in a `List`.
//
// Both answers are a STATUS: 0 accepts, anything else is sent as a plain response and the
// connection is closed. That is one convention for "no" whether the reason is size, type or
// authorisation, and it keeps the response the handler's to write.
@copy pub struct BodySink {
    pub ctx: *mut u8,
    pub on_open: fn(*mut u8, read Exchange) -> i32,
    pub on_data: fn(*mut u8, read []u8) -> i32,
    pub active: bool,
}

// (`body_sink`, not `sink`: `std/sink` is imported here and a module name is taken.)
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn body_sink(ctx: *mut u8, on_open: fn(*mut u8, read Exchange) -> i32,
                                  on_data: fn(*mut u8, read []u8) -> i32) -> BodySink {
    return BodySink{ ctx: ctx, on_open: on_open, on_data: on_data, active: true }
}

// A route with no sink still owns an entry, because the sink table is indexed by route id.
// A fn pointer has no null, so the inactive entry points at two functions that say yes.
@no_alloc @no_os fn sink_open_none(ctx: *mut u8, read x: Exchange) -> i32 { return 0 }
@no_alloc @no_os fn sink_data_none(ctx: *mut u8, read d: []u8) -> i32 { return 0 }

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn no_body_sink() -> BodySink {
    return BodySink{ ctx: null, on_open: &sink_open_none, on_data: &sink_data_none, active: false }
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn matched_route(read x: Exchange) -> i32 { return x.route }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_keep_alive(read x: Exchange) -> bool { return x.keep_alive }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn responded(read x: Exchange) -> bool { return x.sent }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn status_sent(read x: Exchange) -> i32 { return x.status }

// The value bound to `:name` (or `*`) by the matched route, or "".
pub fn param(read x: Exchange, read name: str) -> str {
    let n: usize = list.len(i32, x.params) / 4
    let pb: []u8 = slice(u8, x.pats, x.plen)
    let b: []u8 = slice(u8, x.buf, x.have)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < n {
        let noff: usize = list.get(i32, x.params, i * 4) as usize
        let nlen: usize = list.get(i32, x.params, i * 4 + 1) as usize
        if str_eq(from_utf8(pb[noff .. noff + nlen]), name) {
            let voff: usize = list.get(i32, x.params, i * 4 + 2) as usize
            let vlen: usize = list.get(i32, x.params, i * 4 + 3) as usize
            return http.span_str(http.span(voff, vlen), b)
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return ""
}

// ---- writing the response ----

// The reason phrase for a status, for the handful this server emits itself and the common
// ones a handler will. Unknown codes get a neutral phrase rather than a refusal: the phrase
// carries no meaning to a client, only the number does.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn reason_for(status: i32) -> str {
    if status == 200 { return "OK" }
    if status == 201 { return "Created" }
    if status == 204 { return "No Content" }
    if status == 301 { return "Moved Permanently" }
    if status == 302 { return "Found" }
    if status == 304 { return "Not Modified" }
    if status == 400 { return "Bad Request" }
    if status == 401 { return "Unauthorized" }
    if status == 403 { return "Forbidden" }
    if status == 404 { return "Not Found" }
    if status == 405 { return "Method Not Allowed" }
    if status == 408 { return "Request Timeout" }
    if status == 413 { return "Payload Too Large" }
    if status == 431 { return "Request Header Fields Too Large" }
    if status == 500 { return "Internal Server Error" }
    if status == 503 { return "Service Unavailable" }
    return "Status"
}

// Add a header to the response about to be written. Refused (false) after the status line
// has gone out, and refused for a value that would split the message — `std/http`'s rule.
pub fn set_header(mut x: Exchange, read name: str, read value: str) -> bool {
    if x.sent { return false }
    if http.safe_header_name(name) == false { return false }
    if http.safe_header_value(value) == false { return false }
    string_push(x.hdrs, name)
    string_push(x.hdrs, ": ")
    string_push(x.hdrs, value)
    string_push(x.hdrs, "\r\n")
    return true
}

fn send_bytes_out(mut x: Exchange, read b: []u8) -> bool {
    if x.wctx != null {
        let fine: bool = x.wfn(x.wctx, b)
        if fine { x.bytes_out = x.bytes_out + (b.len as i64) }
        return fine
    }
    let s: sysnet.Socket = sysnet.adopt(x.fd)
    let n: usize = sysnet.send_all(s, b) catch 0
    x.bytes_out = x.bytes_out + (n as i64)
    return n == b.len
}

// Write the status line and headers into the output buffer. `framing` is the one framing
// header the caller chose (a `Content-Length` line or the chunked one).
fn write_head(mut x: Exchange, mut s: Sink, mut ob: []u8, status: i32, read ctype: str) -> bool {
    if http.begin_response(s, ob, status, reason_for(status)) == false { return false }
    sink.put_str(s, ob, string_view(x.hdrs))
    if ctype.len > 0 {
        if http.put_header(s, ob, "Content-Type", ctype) == false { return false }
    }
    if x.keep_alive == false {
        http.put_header(s, ob, "Connection", "close")
    }
    return true
}

// Send a whole response: status, headers, `Content-Length`, body. The body goes out from the
// caller's bytes, not through the output buffer, so it may be larger than the buffer.
pub fn respond(mut x: Exchange, status: i32, read ctype: str, read body: str) -> bool {
    if x.sent { return false }
    var ob: []u8 = slice(u8, x.obuf, x.ocap)
    var s: Sink = sink.new()
    if write_head(x, s, ob, status, ctype) == false { return false }
    http.put_content_length(s, ob, body.len)
    http.end_headers(s, ob)
    if sink.overflowed(s) { return false }
    x.sent = true
    x.status = status
    if send_bytes_out(x, ob[0 .. sink.written(s)]) == false { return false }
    if body.len == 0 { return true }
    let bb: []u8 = slice(u8, body.ptr as *mut u8, body.len)
    return send_bytes_out(x, bb)
}

// Begin a chunked response. Every `stream_write` is one chunk; `stream_end` terminates it.
pub fn stream_begin(mut x: Exchange, status: i32, read ctype: str) -> bool {
    if x.sent { return false }
    var ob: []u8 = slice(u8, x.obuf, x.ocap)
    var s: Sink = sink.new()
    if write_head(x, s, ob, status, ctype) == false { return false }
    http.put_header(s, ob, "Transfer-Encoding", "chunked")
    http.end_headers(s, ob)
    if sink.overflowed(s) { return false }
    x.sent = true
    x.streaming = true
    x.status = status
    return send_bytes_out(x, ob[0 .. sink.written(s)])
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn put_hex_usize(mut s: Sink, mut b: []u8, v: usize) {
    if v == 0 {
        sink.put(s, b, 48 as u8)
        return
    }
    var digits: usize = 0
    var t: usize = v
    for t > 0 {
        digits = digits + 1
        t = t / 16
    }
    var i: usize = digits
    for i > 0 {
        var shift: usize = (i - 1) * 4
        let d: usize = (v >> (shift as u64)) & 15
        if d < 10 { sink.put(s, b, (48 + d) as u8) } else { sink.put(s, b, (87 + d) as u8) }
        i = i - 1
    }
}

// One chunk. An empty write is a no-op rather than a terminator — a zero-length chunk IS the
// terminator on the wire, and a caller writing "" in a loop must not end the body by accident.
pub fn stream_write(mut x: Exchange, read data: str) -> bool {
    if x.streaming == false { return false }
    if data.len == 0 { return true }
    var ob: []u8 = slice(u8, x.obuf, x.ocap)
    var s: Sink = sink.new()
    put_hex_usize(s, ob, data.len)
    sink.put_str(s, ob, "\r\n")
    if send_bytes_out(x, ob[0 .. sink.written(s)]) == false { return false }
    let bb: []u8 = slice(u8, data.ptr as *mut u8, data.len)
    if send_bytes_out(x, bb) == false { return false }
    var s2: Sink = sink.new()
    sink.put_str(s2, ob, "\r\n")
    return send_bytes_out(x, ob[0 .. sink.written(s2)])
}

pub fn stream_end(mut x: Exchange) -> bool {
    if x.streaming == false { return false }
    var ob: []u8 = slice(u8, x.obuf, x.ocap)
    var s: Sink = sink.new()
    sink.put_str(s, ob, "0\r\n\r\n")
    x.streaming = false
    return send_bytes_out(x, ob[0 .. sink.written(s)])
}

// ---- static files ----

// The media type for a file name, by extension. Unknown extensions are bytes.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn content_type_for(read name: str) -> str {
    var dot: usize = name.len
    var i: usize = name.len
    for i > 0 {
        let c: i32 = name[i - 1] as i32
        if c == 46 { dot = i - 1  break }
        if c == 47 { break }
        i = i - 1
    }
    if dot >= name.len { return "application/octet-stream" }
    let ext: str = name[dot + 1 .. name.len]
    if str_eq(ext, "html") { return "text/html; charset=utf-8" }
    if str_eq(ext, "htm") { return "text/html; charset=utf-8" }
    if str_eq(ext, "css") { return "text/css" }
    if str_eq(ext, "js") { return "application/javascript" }
    if str_eq(ext, "json") { return "application/json" }
    if str_eq(ext, "txt") { return "text/plain; charset=utf-8" }
    if str_eq(ext, "svg") { return "image/svg+xml" }
    if str_eq(ext, "png") { return "image/png" }
    if str_eq(ext, "jpg") { return "image/jpeg" }
    if str_eq(ext, "jpeg") { return "image/jpeg" }
    if str_eq(ext, "gif") { return "image/gif" }
    if str_eq(ext, "ico") { return "image/x-icon" }
    return "application/octet-stream"
}

// May `rel` be joined under a root? A path is refused if any segment is `..`, if it carries
// a backslash or a NUL, or if it is absolute after the leading `/` is stripped. This is the
// whole of the traversal defence and it is deliberately a whitelist of shapes rather than a
// normalisation: a path that needs normalising to be safe is one to refuse.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn safe_relative_path(read rel: str) -> bool {
    var i: usize = 0
    var seg_start: usize = 0
    for i <= rel.len {
        var at_end: bool = false
        if i == rel.len { at_end = true }
        var c: i32 = 47
        if at_end == false { c = rel[i] as i32 }
        if c == 0 or c == 92 { return false }
        if c == 47 {
            let seg: str = rel[seg_start .. i]
            if str_eq(seg, "..") { return false }
            seg_start = i + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return true
}

// Serve `<root>/<rel>` with the media type of its extension, or 404. A refused path is a 404
// too — telling a probing client "that exists but you may not" is information it should not
// have. `rel` is normally `param(x, "*")` from a `/static/*` route; an empty `rel` serves
// `index.html`.
pub fn serve_file(mut x: Exchange, mut f: fs.Fs, read root: str, read rel: str) -> bool {
    var start: usize = 0
    for start < rel.len {
        if (rel[start] as i32) != 47 { break }
        start = start + 1
    }
    if start >= rel.len { return serve_named(x, f, root, "index.html") }
    return serve_named(x, f, root, rel[start .. rel.len])
}

fn serve_named(mut x: Exchange, mut f: fs.Fs, read root: str, read r: str) -> bool {
    if safe_relative_path(r) == false { return respond(x, 404, "text/plain", "not found") }
    var p: String = string_new()
    string_push(p, root)
    string_push(p, "/")
    string_push(p, r)
    if fs.present(f, string_view(p)) == false {
        string_free(p)
        return respond(x, 404, "text/plain", "not found")
    }
    var data: String = fs.open_text(f, string_view(p))
    let sent: bool = respond(x, 200, content_type_for(r), string_view(data))
    string_free(data)
    string_free(p)
    return sent
}

// ---- the router ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Patterns are `/`-separated segments; a segment `:name` binds one path segment, and a final
// `*` binds the rest of the path (slashes included). Method `*` matches any method. Routes
// are tried in the order they were added, first match wins — which makes the order the
// author's precedence rule rather than a specificity score they cannot see.

pub struct Router {
    pats: String,
    used: usize,
    routes: List(i32),          // 4 per route: method off, method len, pattern off, pattern len
    handlers: List(Handler),
    sinks: List(BodySink),      // one per route, inactive unless `add_stream_route` set it
}

impl Drop for Router {
    fn drop(mut self) {
        string_free(self.pats)
    }
}

pub fn router(read a: Allocator) -> Router {
    return Router{ pats: string_new(), used: 0, routes: list.make(i32, a),
                   handlers: list.make(Handler, a), sinks: list.make(BodySink, a) }
}

pub fn add_route(mut r: Router, read method: str, read pattern: str, h: Handler) -> i32 {
    let id: i32 = (list.len(i32, r.routes) / 4) as i32
    list.push(i32, r.routes, r.used as i32)
    list.push(i32, r.routes, method.len as i32)
    string_push(r.pats, method)
    r.used = r.used + method.len
    list.push(i32, r.routes, r.used as i32)
    list.push(i32, r.routes, pattern.len as i32)
    string_push(r.pats, pattern)
    r.used = r.used + pattern.len
    list.push(Handler, r.handlers, h)
    list.push(BodySink, r.sinks, no_body_sink())
    return id
}

// The same route, with a sink for its request body. The sink is consulted only when there IS
// a body: a `GET` on a streaming route reaches its handler exactly as it would otherwise.
pub fn add_stream_route(mut r: Router, read method: str, read pattern: str, h: Handler, bs: BodySink) -> i32 {
    let id: i32 = add_route(r, method, pattern, h)
    list.set(BodySink, r.sinks, id as usize, bs)
    return id
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn sink_for(read r: Router, id: i32) -> BodySink {
    if id < 0 { return no_body_sink() }
    if id as usize >= list.len(BodySink, r.sinks) { return no_body_sink() }
    return list.get(BodySink, r.sinks, id as usize)
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn route_count(read r: Router) -> usize { return list.len(i32, r.routes) / 4 }

// Match `pattern` against `path`, pushing params (name off/len into the pattern bytes, value
// off/len into the request buffer — `path_off` is where the path starts there).
fn match_pattern(read pat: str, pat_off: usize, read path: str, path_off: usize, mut params: List(i32)) -> bool {
    var pi: usize = 0
    var ti: usize = 0
    let before: usize = list.len(i32, params)
    for true {
        // The next pattern segment.
        var pe: usize = pi
        for pe < pat.len {
            if (pat[pe] as i32) == 47 { break }
            pe = pe + 1
        }
        var te: usize = ti
        for te < path.len {
            if (path[te] as i32) == 47 { break }
            te = te + 1
        }
        let pseg: str = pat[pi .. pe]
        let tseg: str = path[ti .. te]
        if str_eq(pseg, "*") {
            // The rest of the path, slashes and all.
            list.push(i32, params, (pat_off + pi) as i32)
            list.push(i32, params, 1)
            list.push(i32, params, (path_off + ti) as i32)
            list.push(i32, params, (path.len - ti) as i32)
            return true
        }
        var seg_ok: bool = false
        if pseg.len > 0 {
            if (pseg[0] as i32) == 58 {
                if tseg.len > 0 {
                    list.push(i32, params, (pat_off + pi + 1) as i32)
                    list.push(i32, params, (pseg.len - 1) as i32)
                    list.push(i32, params, (path_off + ti) as i32)
                    list.push(i32, params, tseg.len as i32)
                    seg_ok = true
                }
            } else {
                if str_eq(pseg, tseg) { seg_ok = true }
            }
        } else {
            if tseg.len == 0 { seg_ok = true }
        }
        if seg_ok == false {
            list.truncate(i32, params, before)
            return false
        }
        let p_done: bool = pe >= pat.len
        let t_done: bool = te >= path.len
        if p_done and t_done { return true }
        if p_done or t_done {
            list.truncate(i32, params, before)
            return false
        }
        pi = pe + 1
        ti = te + 1
    }
    return false
}

fn find_route(read r: Router, read method: str, read path: str, path_off: usize, mut params: List(i32)) -> i32 {
    let pv: str = string_view(r.pats)
    let n: usize = list.len(i32, r.routes) / 4
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < n {
        let moff: usize = list.get(i32, r.routes, i * 4) as usize
        let mlen: usize = list.get(i32, r.routes, i * 4 + 1) as usize
        let poff: usize = list.get(i32, r.routes, i * 4 + 2) as usize
        let plen: usize = list.get(i32, r.routes, i * 4 + 3) as usize
        let m: str = pv[moff .. moff + mlen]
        var mok: bool = false
        if str_eq(m, "*") { mok = true }
        if str_eq(m, method) { mok = true }
        if mok {
            if match_pattern(pv[poff .. poff + plen], poff, path, path_off, params) { return i as i32 }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

// ---- the server -------------------------------------------------------------------------

@copy struct Conn {
    fd: i64,
    buf: *mut u8,
    have: usize,
    last: i64,          // clock reading at the last byte received or sent
    served: i64,        // requests answered on this connection
    live: bool,
    // While a request body streams: the head is PINNED at [0, head_len) and the window
    // [head_len, have) holds body bytes not yet handed to the sink. Every field here is
    // `@copy` scalar state on purpose — a `Conn` lives in a raw array, so the exchange (which
    // owns a `String` and a `List`) cannot live here and is rebuilt when the body ends.
    streaming: bool,
    head_len: usize,
    body_left: i64,     // `Content-Length` bytes still owed, or -1 when the body is chunked
    chunks: http.Chunks,
    got: i64,           // body bytes RECEIVED for this message, framing included — the bound
    fed: i64,           // body bytes DELIVERED to the sink, framing excluded — the payload
    sk: BodySink,
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn idle_conn() -> Conn {
    return Conn{ fd: 0 - 1, buf: null, have: 0, last: 0, served: 0, live: false,
                 streaming: false, head_len: 0, body_left: 0, chunks: http.chunks_new(),
                 got: 0, fed: 0, sk: no_body_sink() }
}

fn conn_at(p: *mut Conn, i: usize) -> Conn {
    unsafe { (p + i).* }
}

fn conn_set(p: *mut Conn, i: usize, c: Conn) {
    unsafe { (p + i).* = c }
}

@move pub struct Server {
    r: Router,
    mws: List(Handler),
    lis: sysnet.Socket,
    listening: bool,
    conns: *mut Conn,
    ncap: usize,
    bufcap: usize,
    hs: *mut http.Header,
    obuf: *mut u8,
    ocap: usize,
    lbuf: *mut u8,
    lcap: usize,
    llen: usize,            // the last access-log record's rendered length
    clock: time.Clock,
    lg: log.Logger,
    logging: bool,
    poller: runtime.Poller,
    read_timeout: i64,      // nanos; a partial request older than this is 408 + close
    idle_timeout: i64,      // nanos; a kept-alive connection quiet this long is closed
    max_requests: i64,      // per connection, then `Connection: close`
    max_body: i64,          // bytes RECEIVED for one request body; over it is 413 + close
    accepted: i64,
    served: i64,
    rejected: i64,          // connections refused for want of a table slot
    timeouts: i64,
    bad: i64,               // requests refused by the parser
    oversize: i64,          // request bodies refused with a 413
}

const HTTPD_HEADERS: usize = 100
const HTTPD_OUT_CAP: usize = 16384
const HTTPD_LOG_CAP: usize = 2048
const HTTPD_LINGER_READS: usize = 16    // bufferfuls discarded before an error close
const HTTPD_MAX_BODY: i64 = 8388608     // 8 MiB, the default body bound

// What a `feed` round decided. Three answers rather than a bool, because "the body is
// complete" and "there is nothing more in the buffer yet" are different next steps and a
// server that conflated them would answer a request whose body had not arrived.
const FEED_WAIT: i32 = 0
const FEED_DONE: i32 = 1
const FEED_CLOSE: i32 = 2

// A server over `clock` and `lg`, with room for `max_conns` connections of `buf_cap` bytes
// each. `logging` false keeps the logger but never writes to it — the knob a test that wants
// silence flips, and the shape that keeps a `Logger` a required field rather than an option.
pub fn make(read a: Allocator, take clock: time.Clock, take lg: log.Logger, logging: bool, max_conns: usize, buf_cap: usize) -> Server {
    var conns: *mut Conn = alloc(Conn, max_conns)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < max_conns {
        conn_set(conns, i, idle_conn())
        i = i + 1
    }
    return Server{ r: router(a), mws: list.make(Handler, a), lis: sysnet.closed(), listening: false,
                   conns: conns, ncap: max_conns, bufcap: buf_cap,
                   hs: alloc(http.Header, HTTPD_HEADERS), obuf: alloc(u8, HTTPD_OUT_CAP), ocap: HTTPD_OUT_CAP,
                   lbuf: alloc(u8, HTTPD_LOG_CAP), lcap: HTTPD_LOG_CAP, llen: 0,
                   clock: clock, lg: lg, logging: logging, poller: syspoll.host(),
                   read_timeout: 10000000000, idle_timeout: 30000000000, max_requests: 1000,
                   max_body: HTTPD_MAX_BODY,
                   accepted: 0, served: 0, rejected: 0, timeouts: 0, bad: 0, oversize: 0 }
}

pub fn set_timeouts(mut sv: Server, read_ms: i64, idle_ms: i64) {
    sv.read_timeout = read_ms * 1000000
    sv.idle_timeout = idle_ms * 1000000
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn set_max_requests(mut sv: Server, n: i64) { sv.max_requests = n }

// The largest request body this server will take, counted as bytes RECEIVED — see the header:
// chunk framing is the client's choice, and a bound on the decoded payload alone would leave
// the overhead unbounded.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn set_max_body(mut sv: Server, n: i64) { sv.max_body = n }

pub fn use_middleware(mut sv: Server, h: Handler) {
    list.push(Handler, sv.mws, h)
}

pub fn route(mut sv: Server, read method: str, read pattern: str, h: Handler) -> i32 {
    return add_route(sv.r, method, pattern, h)
}

// A route whose request body is STREAMED to `bs` rather than buffered whole. The sink is
// consulted only when there is a body, so a `GET` on such a route behaves exactly as it would
// on an ordinary one.
pub fn route_stream(mut sv: Server, read method: str, read pattern: str, h: Handler, bs: BodySink) -> i32 {
    return add_stream_route(sv.r, method, pattern, h, bs)
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn accepted(read sv: Server) -> i64 { return sv.accepted }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn served(read sv: Server) -> i64 { return sv.served }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn rejected(read sv: Server) -> i64 { return sv.rejected }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn timeouts(read sv: Server) -> i64 { return sv.timeouts }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn bad_requests(read sv: Server) -> i64 { return sv.bad }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn oversized_bodies(read sv: Server) -> i64 { return sv.oversize }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_listening(read sv: Server) -> bool { return sv.listening }

pub fn live_connections(read sv: Server) -> usize {
    var n: usize = 0
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < sv.ncap {
        if conn_at(sv.conns, i).live { n = n + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return n
}

// `start`/`stop`, not `listen`/`close`: `sysnet` binds both as C symbols, and an extern's
// name is taken in every module that links it.
pub fn start(mut sv: Server, mut net: sysnet.Net, read addr: sysnet.SocketAddr, backlog: i32) -> bool {
    if sv.listening { return false }
    sv.lis = sysnet.listen_on(net, addr, backlog) catch sysnet.closed()
    sv.listening = sysnet.is_open(sv.lis)
    return sv.listening
}

// The port the listener was given — the answer to binding port 0.
pub fn port(read sv: Server) -> u16 {
    if sv.listening == false { return 0 }
    let a: sysnet.SocketAddr = sysnet.local_addr(sv.lis) catch sysnet.addr(0, 0)
    return a.port
}

fn close_conn(mut sv: Server, i: usize) {
    var c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
    if c.live == false { return }
    var s: sysnet.Socket = sysnet.adopt(c.fd)
    sysnet.close_socket(s) catch nothing()
    if c.buf != null { free_ptr(c.buf) }
    conn_set(sv.conns, i, idle_conn())
}

fn nothing() { }

// Close a connection an error response has just been written to, discarding what the client
// has ALREADY sent first.
//
// **Without this the error response is lost.** Closing a socket that still holds unread bytes
// makes the kernel send a RESET rather than a FIN, and a reset discards whatever the client
// has not read yet — so a 413 written to a client half way through a 4 MB upload never
// arrives, and the client sees a dropped connection instead of the reason. That is what
// nginx's `lingering_close` is for.
//
// It is NOT the drain the header refuses. This reads only what is already in the socket, at
// most `HTTPD_LINGER_READS` bufferfuls, and each read is gated on a ZERO-timeout poll — so
// the moment the client stops feeding it, or the cap is reached, the connection closes. The
// cost is bounded by the server's own numbers and not by the client's honesty.
fn linger_close(mut sv: Server, i: usize) {
    var c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
    if c.live == false { return }
    if c.buf != null {
        var fds: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 1)
        var rdy: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, 1)
        unsafe { (fds + 0).* = c.fd }
        let sock: sysnet.Socket = sysnet.adopt(c.fd)
        var cb: []u8 = slice(u8, c.buf, sv.bufcap)
        var k: usize = 0
        for k < HTTPD_LINGER_READS {
            unsafe { (rdy + 0).* = 0 as u8 }
            if runtime.ask(sv.poller, fds, rdy, 1, 0) <= 0 { break }
            var r: u8 = 0
            unsafe { r = (rdy + 0).* }
            if (r as i32) == 0 { break }
            let n: usize = sysnet.recv_bytes(sock, cb) catch 0
            if n == 0 { break }
            k = k + 1
        }
        free_ptr(rdy)
        free_ptr(fds)
    }
    close_conn(sv, i)
}

// Close the listener and every connection. The server may not start again.
pub fn stop(mut sv: Server, mut net: sysnet.Net) {
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < sv.ncap {
        close_conn(sv, i)
        i = i + 1
    }
    if sv.listening {
        sysnet.close_socket(sv.lis) catch nothing()
        sv.listening = false
    }
}

// Release the buffers. Separate from `stop` so a stopped server can still be asked its
// counters; call it last.
pub fn free(mut sv: Server) {
    if sv.conns != null {
        free_ptr(sv.conns as *mut u8)
        sv.conns = null
        sv.ncap = 0
    }
    if sv.hs != null { free_ptr(sv.hs as *mut u8)  sv.hs = null }
    if sv.obuf != null { free_ptr(sv.obuf)  sv.obuf = null }
    if sv.lbuf != null { free_ptr(sv.lbuf)  sv.lbuf = null }
    log.free(sv.lg)
}

// Send a minimal response with no handler involved — the parser's refusals and the read
// timeout.
fn send_plain(mut sv: Server, fd: i64, status: i32, read text: str) -> i64 {
    var ob: []u8 = slice(u8, sv.obuf, sv.ocap)
    var s: Sink = sink.new()
    http.begin_response(s, ob, status, reason_for(status))
    http.put_header(s, ob, "Content-Type", "text/plain")
    http.put_content_length(s, ob, text.len)
    http.put_header(s, ob, "Connection", "close")
    http.end_headers(s, ob)
    sink.put_str(s, ob, text)
    let sock: sysnet.Socket = sysnet.adopt(fd)
    let n: usize = sysnet.send_all(sock, ob[0 .. sink.written(s)]) catch 0
    return n as i64
}

// The access-log record for one exchange.
fn access_log(mut sv: Server, read x: Exchange, ms: i64) {
    if sv.logging == false { return }
    if log.begin(sv.lg, log.LOG_INFO, "request") == false { return }
    log.kv_str(sv.lg, "method", method(x))
    log.kv_str(sv.lg, "path", path(x))
    log.kv_i64(sv.lg, "status", x.status as i64)
    log.kv_i64(sv.lg, "bytes", x.bytes_out)
    log.kv_i64(sv.lg, "ms", ms)
    var lb: []u8 = slice(u8, sv.lbuf, sv.lcap)
    var s: Sink = sink.new()
    log.end(sv.lg, s, lb)
    sv.llen = sink.written(s)
}

// The last access-log record as rendered (logfmt or JSON, per the logger), or "" when
// logging is off or nothing has been served. What the writer received; a test reads it
// here rather than through a buffered writer it cannot see into.
pub fn last_log(read sv: Server) -> str {
    let lb: []u8 = slice(u8, sv.lbuf, sv.lcap)
    return from_utf8(lb[0 .. sv.llen])
}

// Does `req` ask to keep the connection? HTTP/1.1 keeps unless `close`; HTTP/1.0 closes
// unless `keep-alive`.
fn wants_keep_alive(read b: []u8, read hs: []http.Header, read req: http.Request) -> bool {
    let cv: str = http.header_value(hs, req.headers, b, "connection")
    if eq_ci_str(cv, "close") { return false }
    if req.minor == 0 {
        return eq_ci_str(cv, "keep-alive")
    }
    return true
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn eq_ci_str(read a: str, read b: str) -> bool {
    if a.len != b.len { return false }
    for i in 0..a.len {
        var c: i32 = a[i] as i32
        var d: i32 = b[i] as i32
        if c >= 65 and c <= 90 { c = c + 32 }
        if d >= 65 and d <= 90 { d = d + 32 }
        if c != d { return false }
    }
    return true
}

// The exchange for one request on connection `i`. BOTH paths build it here — the buffered one
// for its handler, the streaming one for its sink's `on_open` — so what a sink is shown of a
// request and what the handler is shown are the same view, decided in one place.
fn build_exchange(mut sv: Server, i: usize, read req: http.Request, nhs: usize, streamed: i64) -> Exchange {
    let c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
    return build_exchange_from(sv, c.buf, c.have, req, nhs, streamed, c.fd, c.served, null, &no_write)
}

// The exchange over bytes at `buf[0 .. have]`, whatever transport they came by. `served` is
// how many requests the connection has answered before this one (the keep-alive bound);
// `wctx`/`wfn` the transport hook, null for the descriptor.
fn build_exchange_from(mut sv: Server, buf: *mut u8, have: usize, read req: http.Request, nhs: usize, streamed: i64,
                       fd: i64, served: i64, wctx: *mut u8, wfn: fn(*mut u8, read []u8) -> bool) -> Exchange {
    let b: []u8 = slice(u8, buf, have)
    let hs: []http.Header = slice(http.Header, sv.hs, nhs)

    // Split the target at `?`.
    var qoff: usize = req.target.off + req.target.len
    var k: usize = req.target.off
    for k < req.target.off + req.target.len {
        if (b[k] as i32) == 63 { qoff = k  break }
        k = k + 1
    }
    var qspan: http.Span = http.span(qoff, 0)
    if qoff < req.target.off + req.target.len {
        qspan = http.span(qoff + 1, req.target.off + req.target.len - qoff - 1)
    }

    var keep: bool = wants_keep_alive(b, hs, req)
    if served + 1 >= sv.max_requests { keep = false }

    let pv: str = string_view(sv.r.pats)
    return Exchange{ buf: buf, have: have, req: req, hs: sv.hs, nhs: nhs,
                     bbuf: null, blen: 0, streamed: streamed,
                     route: 0 - 1, params: list.make(i32, mem.system_allocator()),
                     pats: pv.ptr as *mut u8, plen: pv.len,
                     path: http.span(req.target.off, qoff - req.target.off), query: qspan,
                     fd: fd, status: 0, hdrs: string_new(), sent: false, streaming: false,
                     bytes_out: 0, keep_alive: keep, obuf: sv.obuf, ocap: sv.ocap,
                     wctx: wctx, wfn: wfn }
}

// A minimal response through the transport hook — `send_plain`'s twin for bytes that did
// not come over a descriptor.
fn plain_via(mut sv: Server, wctx: *mut u8, wfn: fn(*mut u8, read []u8) -> bool, status: i32, read text: str) {
    var ob: []u8 = slice(u8, sv.obuf, sv.ocap)
    var s: Sink = sink.new()
    http.begin_response(s, ob, status, reason_for(status))
    http.put_header(s, ob, "Content-Type", "text/plain")
    http.put_content_length(s, ob, text.len)
    http.put_header(s, ob, "Connection", "close")
    http.end_headers(s, ob)
    sink.put_str(s, ob, text)
    wfn(wctx, ob[0 .. sink.written(s)])
}

// What one answer through another transport reported.
@copy pub struct Answered {
    pub consumed: i64,     // bytes of the input the message took; 0 incomplete; -1 refused
    pub keep: bool,        // the client asked to keep the connection, and the bound allows it
    pub status: i32,       // the status written, 0 when nothing was
}

// Answer ONE request that arrived through ANOTHER transport — `std/httpds` reads TLS records
// into its own buffer and hands the bytes here. `b` is what has arrived so far; `fd` is the
// descriptor for the access log's sake only (nothing is read from or written to it); every
// response byte goes through `wfn(wctx, …)`; `served` is how many requests this connection
// has answered before, for the keep-alive bound; `full` says the caller's buffer can grow no
// further, so an incomplete message is refused rather than waited for; `close` forces
// `Connection: close` on the answer. `consumed` is how
// many bytes the answered message took; 0 means incomplete (read more); -1 means refused,
// with the error response already written through the hook — close. Bodies are buffered
// whole: a route registered with a body sink is answered from the buffer, its sink not
// consulted, and a body over `max_body` is 413 as on the descriptor path.
pub fn answer_bytes(mut sv: Server, read b: []u8, fd: i64, wctx: *mut u8, wfn: fn(*mut u8, read []u8) -> bool,
                    served: i64, full: bool, close: bool) -> Answered {
    var ans: Answered = Answered{ consumed: 0, keep: false, status: 0 }
    if b.len == 0 { return ans }
    var hs: []http.Header = slice(http.Header, sv.hs, HTTPD_HEADERS)
    var h: http.Head = http.empty_head()
    let ho: i32 = http.parse_head(b, hs, h)
    if ho == http.HTTP_INCOMPLETE {
        if full {
            sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
            plain_via(sv, wctx, wfn, 431, "request too large")
            ans.consumed = 0 - 1
            ans.status = 431
        }
        return ans
    }
    if ho != http.HTTP_OK {
        sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
        var st: i32 = 400
        if ho == http.HTTP_TOO_LARGE { st = 431 }
        plain_via(sv, wctx, wfn, st, http.outcome_name(ho))
        ans.consumed = 0 - 1
        ans.status = st
        return ans
    }
    if h.length > sv.max_body {
        sv.oversize = sv.oversize + 1
        plain_via(sv, wctx, wfn, 413, "body too large")
        ans.consumed = 0 - 1
        ans.status = 413
        return ans
    }
    var req: http.Request = http.empty_request()
    let o: i32 = http.body_from_head(b, h, req)
    if o == http.HTTP_INCOMPLETE {
        if full {
            sv.oversize = sv.oversize + 1
            plain_via(sv, wctx, wfn, 413, "body too large")
            ans.consumed = 0 - 1
            ans.status = 413
        }
        return ans
    }
    if o != http.HTTP_OK {
        sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
        var st2: i32 = 400
        if o == http.HTTP_TOO_LARGE { st2 = 431 }
        plain_via(sv, wctx, wfn, st2, http.outcome_name(o))
        ans.consumed = 0 - 1
        ans.status = st2
        return ans
    }
    let t0: i64 = time.read_nanos(sv.clock)
    var x: Exchange = build_exchange_from(sv, b.ptr as *mut u8, b.len, req, req.headers, 0, fd, served, wctx, wfn)
    // `close` forces `Connection: close` on the answer — a transport that serves one
    // request per connection says so on the wire rather than leaving the client to find out.
    if close { x.keep_alive = false }
    dispatch(sv, x, b, req, t0)
    ans.consumed = req.total as i64
    ans.keep = x.keep_alive
    ans.status = x.status
    return ans
}

// Answer one complete request at the front of connection `i`'s buffer. Returns true when the
// connection stays open. `streamed` is how many body bytes already went to a sink — zero for
// every request whose body was buffered.
fn answer(mut sv: Server, i: usize, read req: http.Request, nhs: usize, streamed: i64) -> bool {
    var c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
    let t0: i64 = time.read_nanos(sv.clock)
    let b: []u8 = slice(u8, c.buf, c.have)
    var x: Exchange = build_exchange(sv, i, req, nhs, streamed)
    dispatch(sv, x, b, req, t0)

    // Consume the request: whatever follows is the next one.
    let rest: usize = c.have - req.total
    var j: usize = 0
    var cb: []u8 = slice(u8, c.buf, sv.bufcap)
    for j < rest {
        cb[j] = cb[req.total + j]
        j = j + 1
    }
    c.have = rest
    c.served = c.served + 1
    c.last = time.read_nanos(sv.clock)
    conn_set(sv.conns, i, c)
    return x.keep_alive
}

// Everything after the exchange is built — the chunked decode, the middleware, the route, the
// fallbacks, the access log — shared by the descriptor path and the bytes-in path, so what a
// transport cannot change is decided once.
fn dispatch(mut sv: Server, mut x: Exchange, read b: []u8, read req: http.Request, t0: i64) {
    // A chunked body is decoded once, up front, into its own buffer.
    if req.chunked {
        var dec: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, req.body.len + 1)
        var db: []u8 = slice(u8, dec, req.body.len + 1)
        let n: isize = http.decode_chunked(b, req.body, db)
        if n >= 0 {
            x.bbuf = dec
            x.blen = n as usize
        } else {
            free_ptr(dec)
        }
    }

    // Middleware, in order. One that answers DONE has written the response.
    var halted: bool = false
    var mi: usize = 0
    for mi < list.len(Handler, sv.mws) {
        let h: Handler = list.get(Handler, sv.mws, mi)
        let v: i32 = h.f(h.ctx, x)
        if v == HTTPD_DONE { halted = true  break }
        mi = mi + 1
    }

    if halted == false {
        x.route = find_route(sv.r, method(x), path(x), req.target.off, x.params)
        if x.route >= 0 {
            let h2: Handler = list.get(Handler, sv.r.handlers, x.route as usize)
            h2.f(h2.ctx, x)
            if x.streaming { stream_end(x) }
            if x.sent == false { respond(x, 500, "text/plain", "the handler sent nothing") }
        } else {
            respond(x, 404, "text/plain", "not found")
        }
    } else {
        if x.streaming { stream_end(x) }
        if x.sent == false { respond(x, 500, "text/plain", "the middleware sent nothing") }
    }

    let t1: i64 = time.read_nanos(sv.clock)
    access_log(sv, x, (t1 - t0) / 1000000)
    sv.served = sv.served + 1

    if x.bbuf != null { free_ptr(x.bbuf) }
    string_free(x.hdrs)
}

// ---- streaming a request body in --------------------------------------------------------

// Move `n` consumed body bytes out of the window, keeping the head pinned at [0, head_len).
// Returns the new `have`. This is the same memmove the buffered path does when it consumes a
// message, and it is what leaves the buffer sitting exactly at the next request's first byte
// the moment a body ends.
@no_alloc @no_os fn slid(buf: *mut u8, cap: usize, head_len: usize, have: usize, n: usize) -> usize {
    var cb: []u8 = slice(u8, buf, cap)
    let rest: usize = have - head_len - n
    var j: usize = 0
    for j < rest {
        cb[head_len + j] = cb[head_len + n + j]
        j = j + 1
    }
    return head_len + rest
}

// The sink of the route this head matches, or the inactive one. Routing happens AGAIN later
// against the exchange's own parameter list; this pass throws its parameters away, because
// the buffer they point into is about to be rewritten by the body.
fn sink_for_head(mut sv: Server, read b: []u8, read h: http.Head) -> BodySink {
    var qoff: usize = h.target.off + h.target.len
    var k: usize = h.target.off
    for k < h.target.off + h.target.len {
        if (b[k] as i32) == 63 { qoff = k  break }
        k = k + 1
    }
    let m: str = http.span_str(h.method, b)
    let p: str = http.span_str(http.span(h.target.off, qoff - h.target.off), b)
    var params: List(i32) = list.make(i32, mem.system_allocator())
    let id: i32 = find_route(sv.r, m, p, h.target.off, params)
    let bs: BodySink = sink_for(sv.r, id)
    list.free(i32, params)
    return bs
}

// The head of a request with a body is in and its route has a sink. Show the sink the head,
// and if it accepts, PIN the head and put the connection into the streaming state.
fn open_stream(mut sv: Server, i: usize, read h: http.Head, bs: BodySink) -> bool {
    var req: http.Request = http.empty_request()
    req.method = h.method
    req.target = h.target
    req.minor = h.minor
    req.headers = h.headers
    req.body = http.span(h.body_off, 0)
    req.total = h.body_off
    var x: Exchange = build_exchange(sv, i, req, h.headers, 0)
    let v: i32 = bs.on_open(bs.ctx, x)
    string_free(x.hdrs)
    list.free(i32, x.params)

    var c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
    if v != 0 {
        sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
        send_plain(sv, c.fd, v, reason_for(v))
        return false
    }
    c.streaming = true
    c.head_len = h.body_off
    c.body_left = 0 - 1
    if h.chunked == false { c.body_left = h.length }
    c.chunks = http.chunks_new()
    c.got = 0
    c.fed = 0
    c.sk = bs
    conn_set(sv.conns, i, c)
    return true
}

// Hand connection `i`'s sink whatever body bytes the buffer holds, then slide what is left
// back against the pinned head. One call moves exactly what has ARRIVED and then returns to
// the readiness loop — there is no wait here, which is why a slow uploader costs one buffer
// and not the thread.
fn feed(mut sv: Server, i: usize) -> i32 {
    var c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
    var cb: []u8 = slice(u8, c.buf, sv.bufcap)

    if c.body_left >= 0 {
        // `Content-Length`: exactly that many bytes and never one more, so the bytes after the
        // body are the NEXT request's and can never be stolen into this one.
        var n: usize = c.have - c.head_len
        if (n as i64) > c.body_left { n = c.body_left as usize }
        if n > 0 {
            let v: i32 = c.sk.on_data(c.sk.ctx, cb[c.head_len .. c.head_len + n])
            if v != 0 {
                sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
                send_plain(sv, c.fd, v, reason_for(v))
                return FEED_CLOSE
            }
            c.body_left = c.body_left - (n as i64)
            c.got = c.got + (n as i64)
            c.fed = c.fed + (n as i64)
            c.have = slid(c.buf, sv.bufcap, c.head_len, c.have, n)
            conn_set(sv.conns, i, c)
        }
        if c.body_left == 0 { return FEED_DONE }
        return FEED_WAIT
    }

    // `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, decoded one unit at a time by `std/http` over a window
    // that is refilled underneath it.
    for true {
        var st: http.Chunks = c.chunks
        var g: http.Span = http.span(0, 0)
        var used: usize = 0
        let o: i32 = http.chunk_step(cb[c.head_len .. c.have], 0, st, g, used)
        if o == http.HTTP_INCOMPLETE {
            if c.have >= sv.bufcap {
                // A chunk-size line or a chunk's trailing CRLF that does not fit: waiting
                // cannot help, because there is nowhere left to put the rest of it.
                sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
                send_plain(sv, c.fd, 400, "chunk framing too large")
                return FEED_CLOSE
            }
            return FEED_WAIT
        }
        if o != http.HTTP_OK {
            // A malformed chunk size is a 400 and a close — not a wait for bytes that could
            // never make it valid.
            sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
            send_plain(sv, c.fd, 400, "bad chunked body")
            return FEED_CLOSE
        }
        c.chunks = st
        c.got = c.got + (used as i64)
        if c.got > sv.max_body {
            sv.oversize = sv.oversize + 1
            send_plain(sv, c.fd, 413, "body too large")
            return FEED_CLOSE
        }
        if g.len > 0 {
            c.fed = c.fed + (g.len as i64)
            let v2: i32 = c.sk.on_data(c.sk.ctx, cb[c.head_len + g.off .. c.head_len + g.off + g.len])
            if v2 != 0 {
                sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
                send_plain(sv, c.fd, v2, reason_for(v2))
                return FEED_CLOSE
            }
        }
        if used > 0 { c.have = slid(c.buf, sv.bufcap, c.head_len, c.have, used) }
        conn_set(sv.conns, i, c)
        if http.chunks_done(c.chunks) { return FEED_DONE }
        if used == 0 { return FEED_WAIT }
    }
    return FEED_WAIT
}

// The body is complete. The head is still PINNED at the front of the buffer, so it is parsed
// again — which refills the server's shared header array, that another connection may have
// used while this body was arriving — and answered. `req.total` is the head's length, so
// `answer`'s consume step moves what follows (a pipelined next request) to the front.
fn finish_stream(mut sv: Server, i: usize) -> bool {
    var c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
    let streamed: i64 = c.fed
    let head_len: usize = c.head_len
    var hs: []http.Header = slice(http.Header, sv.hs, HTTPD_HEADERS)
    var h: http.Head = http.empty_head()
    let o: i32 = http.parse_head(slice(u8, c.buf, head_len), hs, h)
    if o != http.HTTP_OK {
        // Unreachable by construction — the same bytes parsed once already — and answered
        // rather than asserted, because a server that trusts its own invariant silently is one
        // that cannot say what went wrong.
        sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
        send_plain(sv, c.fd, 400, "bad request")
        return false
    }
    var req: http.Request = http.empty_request()
    req.method = h.method
    req.target = h.target
    req.minor = h.minor
    req.headers = h.headers
    req.body = http.span(head_len, 0)
    req.total = head_len

    c.streaming = false
    c.head_len = 0
    c.body_left = 0
    c.chunks = http.chunks_new()
    c.got = 0
    c.fed = 0
    c.sk = no_body_sink()
    conn_set(sv.conns, i, c)
    return answer(sv, i, req, h.headers, streamed)
}

// Refuse a body the buffer cannot hold. See the header: it is 413 and a CLOSE, not a drain —
// the only bound on how long draining would take is the client's own honesty.
fn refuse_body(mut sv: Server, fd: i64) -> bool {
    sv.oversize = sv.oversize + 1
    send_plain(sv, fd, 413, "body too large")
    return false
}

// Parse and answer every complete request in connection `i`'s buffer, feeding a streaming
// body along the way. Returns true when the connection stays open.
fn drain(mut sv: Server, i: usize) -> bool {
    for true {
        var c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
        if c.streaming {
            let s: i32 = feed(sv, i)
            if s == FEED_CLOSE { return false }
            if s == FEED_WAIT { return true }
            if finish_stream(sv, i) == false { return false }
            continue
        }
        if c.have == 0 { return true }
        var hs: []http.Header = slice(http.Header, sv.hs, HTTPD_HEADERS)
        let b: []u8 = slice(u8, c.buf, c.have)

        // The HEAD first, always: a body cannot be routed on, bounded or streamed until the
        // framing scan has said which of the two framings it obeys.
        var h: http.Head = http.empty_head()
        let ho: i32 = http.parse_head(b, hs, h)
        if ho == http.HTTP_INCOMPLETE {
            if c.have >= sv.bufcap {
                // The HEAD alone fills the buffer. Still 431, not 413: that is a header bound,
                // and a client told 413 would trim the wrong thing.
                sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
                send_plain(sv, c.fd, 431, "request too large")
                return false
            }
            return true
        }
        if ho != http.HTTP_OK {
            sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
            var st: i32 = 400
            if ho == http.HTTP_TOO_LARGE { st = 431 }
            send_plain(sv, c.fd, st, http.outcome_name(ho))
            return false
        }
        // A DECLARED length over the bound is refused before one body byte is waited for.
        if h.length > sv.max_body { return refuse_body(sv, c.fd) }

        var bs: BodySink = no_body_sink()
        if h.chunked or h.length > 0 { bs = sink_for_head(sv, b, h) }
        if bs.active {
            if open_stream(sv, i, h, bs) == false { return false }
            continue
        }

        // Not streamed: the whole message has to fit the buffer.
        var req: http.Request = http.empty_request()
        let o: i32 = http.body_from_head(b, h, req)
        if o == http.HTTP_INCOMPLETE {
            if h.chunked == false and h.length >= 0 {
                if h.body_off + (h.length as usize) > sv.bufcap { return refuse_body(sv, c.fd) }
            }
            if c.have >= sv.bufcap { return refuse_body(sv, c.fd) }
            return true
        }
        if o != http.HTTP_OK {
            sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
            var st2: i32 = 400
            if o == http.HTTP_TOO_LARGE { st2 = 431 }
            send_plain(sv, c.fd, st2, http.outcome_name(o))
            return false
        }
        if answer(sv, i, req, req.headers, 0) == false { return false }
    }
    return true
}

fn accept_ready(mut sv: Server, mut net: sysnet.Net) {
    var s: sysnet.Socket = sysnet.accept_one(net, sv.lis) catch sysnet.closed()
    if sysnet.is_open(s) == false { return }
    sv.accepted = sv.accepted + 1
    var slot: usize = sv.ncap
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < sv.ncap {
        if conn_at(sv.conns, i).live == false { slot = i  break }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if slot >= sv.ncap {
        // No room. Refuse with a 503 rather than leaving the client to time out: the
        // table is a deliberate bound, and a bound that is silent is a hang.
        sv.rejected = sv.rejected + 1
        send_plain(sv, s.fd, 503, "too many connections")
        sysnet.close_socket(s) catch nothing()
        return
    }
    let now: i64 = time.read_nanos(sv.clock)
    var nc: Conn = idle_conn()
    nc.fd = s.fd
    nc.buf = alloc(u8, sv.bufcap)
    nc.last = now
    nc.live = true
    conn_set(sv.conns, slot, nc)
}

fn read_ready(mut sv: Server, i: usize) {
    var c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
    if c.have >= sv.bufcap {
        // `drain` refuses a full buffer itself, so this is only reachable if it ever stopped
        // doing so. The answer still has to be the right one for what filled it.
        if c.streaming {
            refuse_body(sv, c.fd)
        } else {
            sv.bad = sv.bad + 1
            send_plain(sv, c.fd, 431, "request too large")
        }
        linger_close(sv, i)
        return
    }
    var cb: []u8 = slice(u8, c.buf, sv.bufcap)
    var win: []u8 = cb[c.have .. sv.bufcap]
    let sock: sysnet.Socket = sysnet.adopt(c.fd)
    // A failed read and a clean close both end the connection; the distinction does not
    // change what a server can do with the socket.
    let n: usize = sysnet.recv_bytes(sock, win) catch 0
    if n == 0 {
        close_conn(sv, i)
        return
    }
    c.have = c.have + n
    c.last = time.read_nanos(sv.clock)
    conn_set(sv.conns, i, c)
    if drain(sv, i) == false { linger_close(sv, i) }
}

fn expire(mut sv: Server) {
    let now: i64 = time.read_nanos(sv.clock)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < sv.ncap {
        let c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
        if c.live {
            if c.have > 0 {
                if now - c.last > sv.read_timeout {
                    sv.timeouts = sv.timeouts + 1
                    send_plain(sv, c.fd, 408, "request timeout")
                    linger_close(sv, i)
                }
            } else {
                if now - c.last > sv.idle_timeout {
                    sv.timeouts = sv.timeouts + 1
                    close_conn(sv, i)
                }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// One round: wait up to `wait_ms` for something readable, then do everything that is ready
// and expire what has timed out. Returns the requests answered in this round, or -1 when the
// server is not listening.
pub fn step(mut sv: Server, mut net: sysnet.Net, wait_ms: i64) -> i64 {
    if sv.listening == false { return 0 - 1 }
    let before: i64 = sv.served
    let n: usize = sv.ncap + 1
    var fds: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, n)
    var rdy: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, n)
    var idx: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, n)
    unsafe { (fds + 0).* = sv.lis.fd }
    unsafe { (idx + 0).* = 0 - 1 }
    var m: usize = 1
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < sv.ncap {
        let c: Conn = conn_at(sv.conns, i)
        if c.live {
            unsafe { (fds + m).* = c.fd }
            unsafe { (idx + m).* = i as i64 }
            m = m + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    var k: usize = 0
    for k < m {
        unsafe { (rdy + k).* = 0 as u8 }
        k = k + 1
    }
    var wait: i64 = wait_ms * 1000000
    if wait < 0 { wait = 0 }
    let got: i64 = runtime.ask(sv.poller, fds, rdy, m, wait)
    if got > 0 {
        var j: usize = 0
        for j < m {
            var r: u8 = 0
            unsafe { r = (rdy + j).* }
            if (r as i32) != 0 {
                var which: i64 = 0
                unsafe { which = (idx + j).* }
                if which < 0 { accept_ready(sv, net) } else { read_ready(sv, which as usize) }
            }
            j = j + 1
        }
    }
    expire(sv)
    free_ptr(idx)
    free_ptr(rdy)
    free_ptr(fds)
    return sv.served - before
}

// Step until `budget_ms` of waiting has been spent. Returns the requests answered. The wait
// per step is bounded by what is left of the budget, so a quiet server returns on time.
pub fn serve_for(mut sv: Server, mut net: sysnet.Net, budget_ms: i64) -> i64 {
    let start: i64 = time.read_nanos(sv.clock)
    var total: i64 = 0
    for true {
        let spent: i64 = (time.read_nanos(sv.clock) - start) / 1000000
        let left: i64 = budget_ms - spent
        if left <= 0 { break }
        var w: i64 = left
        if w > 50 { w = 50 }
        let n: i64 = step(sv, net, w)
        if n < 0 { break }
        total = total + n
    }
    return total
}
